scripts/social.sh now keeps a lasting record of each run's spend. It appends one line per run to phases/social/spend.jsonl on the audit-log branch. When a run left no usage record, the line says "usage":"no_terminal_emit" instead of a zero.

What I checked myself:
• Harness tests: bash tests/harness_logic.sh gave 249 passed, 0 failed. That includes all the new social sink cases, (a) through (f).
• Syntax: bash -n scripts/social.sh is clean.
• Variables: DAY, AGENT_EXIT and SESSION_TIME are all set in social.sh, so the row isn't built from empty values.
• Protected files: social.sh is not on evolve.sh's protected list, so editing it was allowed.

Other points from reading the diff:
• Main checkout untouched: the write happens in a separate temporary worktree that is removed before the script's later commit on main. Test (e) runs against a main with uncommitted changes and confirms its status, index and HEAD are unchanged.
• Failures don't break the script: every failed step prints one stderr line naming the step and returns 0, so it never changes the script's exit status. It still works under the script's set -euo pipefail.
• Missing branch: if the remote has no audit-log branch, it prints one skip line and changes nothing.
• Retry: if the first push is rejected, it fetches, rebases and pushes once more. Test (f) covers this.
• Both exits: it runs on the API-error exit and the normal exit.
• Records: the change is written up in ARCHITECTURE.md. Nothing in the diff touches product behaviour.

One thing the tests can't show: whether the social workflow's CI credentials are allowed to push to audit-log. If they aren't, the sink fails without breaking the run, and the first real social run will show which happened.
